Burundi.text_english:
Article 2
For the purposes of this law and in accordance with the Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court and the Convention of 9 December 1948 on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide, genocide is defined as any of the following acts committed with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnic, racial, or religious group as such:
(a) killing members of the group;
(b) causing serious bodily or mental harm to members of the group;
(c) deliberately inflicting on the group conditions of life calculated to bring about its physical destruction in whole or in part;
(d) imposing measures intended to prevent births within the group;
(e) forcibly transferring children of the group to another group.

Cabo Verde.text_english:
Article 268.
(Genocide)

Whoever, in the execution of a concerted plan, and with the intention of destroying, in whole or in part, a national, ethnic, racial, religious or other group, determined according to any arbitrary criteria, commits in relation to members of that group any of the following acts, shall be punished with a prison sentence of 15 to 25 years:

a) Murder or serious bodily or mental injury;

b) Deportation, enslavement or abduction followed by disappearance;

c) Subjection to systematic and widespread practices of torture or cruel, degrading and inhuman treatment, likely to bring about the destruction, in whole or in part, of the group;

d) Measures preventing procreation or births;

e) Forced transfer of children of one group to another.

