Benin.text_english:
Article 464: Anyone who commits,
as part of a widespread or systematic attack directed against any
civilian population and with knowledge of the intent of such an attack, any
of the following acts is guilty of a crime against humanity:

- murder;

- extermination;

- enslavement;

- deportation or forcible transfer of population;

- imprisonment or other severe deprivation of physical liberty in violation
of fundamental provisions of international law;

- torture;

- rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, enforced pregnancy, enforced sterilization or

any other form of sexual violence of comparable gravity;

- persecution of any identifiable group or collectivity on
political, racial, national, ethnic, cultural, religious or gender grounds as defined in
paragraph 3, or on other grounds universally recognized as
impermissible under international law, in connection with any act referred to in
this paragraph or any crime within the jurisdiction of the Court;

- enforced disappearances of persons;

- the crime of apartheid;

- other inhumane acts of a similar character intentionally causing great suffering or serious injury to physical or mental health.

Article 465: An attack against a civilian population is defined as the conduct of multiple acts referred to in the preceding article against any civilian population, in application of or in pursuit of the policy of a State or organization having as its object such an attack;

- an act of extermination is the intentional imposition of living conditions, such as the deprivation of access to food and medicine, calculated to bring about the destruction of a part of the population;

- constitutes an act of enslavement, the exercise over a person
any or all of the powers associated with property rights, including
in the context of trafficking in human beings, particularly women and
children;

- constitutes an act of deportation or forcible transfer of population, the forcible removal of persons, by expulsion or other coercive means, from the area where they are lawfully located, without grounds recognized under international law;

- constitutes an act of torture, the intentional infliction of severe pain or suffering, whether physical or mental, on a person in one's custody or control; the meaning of this term does not extend to pain or suffering arising solely from, inherent in, or incidental to lawful sanctions;

- constitutes an act of forced pregnancy, the unlawful detention of a woman forcibly impregnated, with the intent to alter the ethnic composition of a population or to commit other serious violations of international law.

This definition may in no way be interpreted as having an impact on national laws relating to pregnancy;

- constitutes an act of persecution, the intentional and serious denial of fundamental rights in violation of international law, on grounds related to the identity of the group or community targeted;

- constitutes a crime of apartheid, inhumane acts similar to those referred to in Article 464, committed within the framework of an institutionalized regime of systematic oppression and domination of one racial group over any other racial group or groups and with the intent to maintain that regime;

- constitutes an act of enforced disappearance of persons, cases where persons
are arrested, detained or abducted by a State or political organization or
with the authorization, support or assent of that State or organization,
which then refuses to admit that those persons are deprived of their liberty or to
disclose their fate or whereabouts, with the intention
of removing them from the protection of the law for a prolonged period.

