Democratic Republic of the Congo.text_english:
Article 222
For the purposes of this Act, “crime against humanity” means any of the following acts when committed as part of a widespread or systematic attack directed against any civilian population and with knowledge of such attack:
1. murder;

2. extermination, including the intentional imposition of living conditions, such as the deprivation of access to food and medicine, calculated to bring about the destruction of part of the population;

3. enslavement, including the exercise over any person of any or all of the powers associated with the right of ownership, including in the context of trafficking in human beings, particularly women and children;

4. Deportation or forcible transfer of population,
understood as the forcible removal of
persons, by expulsion or other coercive means, from the area where they are legally located, without grounds permitted under international law;

5. Imprisonment or other severe deprivation of physical liberty in violation of fundamental provisions of international law;

6. Torture, understood as the intentional infliction of severe pain or suffering, whether physical or mental, on a person in their custody or control;

This term does not include pain or suffering arising solely from, inherent in, or incidental to lawful sanctions;

7. Forced pregnancy, understood as the unlawful detention
of a woman forcibly impregnated, with the intent to alter the ethnic composition of a
population or to commit other serious violations
of international law. This definition may not
in any way be interpreted as having an impact on national laws relating to
pregnancy;

8. Rape, sexual slavery, forced prostitution,
forced sterilization, or any other form of sexual violence of similar gravity;

9. Persecution of any identifiable group or collectivity
on political, racial,
national, ethnic, tribal, cultural, religious, or gender grounds
or on other grounds universally
recognized as inadmissible under international law, in connection with any act referred to in
this paragraph.

“Persecution” means the intentional and serious denial of fundamental rights in violation of international law, on grounds related to the identity of the group or community targeted;

10. Enforced disappearances of persons, understood as cases where persons are arrested, detained, or abducted by a State or political organization, or with the authorization, support, or consent of that State or organization, which then refuses to acknowledge that such persons are deprived of their liberty or to disclose their fate or whereabouts, with the intention of shielding them from the protection of the law for a prolonged period;

11. The crime of apartheid, understood as inhumane acts similar to those covered by this article, committed within the framework of an institutionalized regime of systematic oppression and domination of one racial group over any other racial group or groups, and with the intention of maintaining that regime;

12. Other inhumane acts of a similar character intentionally causing great suffering or serious injury to body or to physical or mental health.
Crimes against humanity are punishable by death.

sections.text_english:
ARTICLE 385
(War crimes against civilians)
1. Anyone who, in violation of international law and in the context of an internal or international armed conflict or military occupation of a State, territory or part thereof, shall be punished with imprisonment for 5 to 16 years, unless a more severe penalty is applicable under another penal provision:

a) Attacks the civilian population;

b) Takes hostages from the civilian population;

c) Recruits or allows the recruitment and service in the belligerent forces of minors under the age of 16;

d) Uses civilians or other persons protected by international law to prevent certain places, areas or forces from being targeted by military operations, using them as human shields;

e) To compel nationals of an enemy power to fight or participate in military operations against their own country or to force members of the civilian population to enlist and fight in a belligerent force in an internal armed conflict;

f) To intentionally launch an attack, knowing that it will cause injuries and loss of human life among the civilian population, clearly excessive in relation to the expected military advantages;

g) To attack personnel on a mission of peacekeeping or humanitarian assistance, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, whenever such personnel are entitled to the protection afforded to civilians by international law.

2. For the purposes of this article, civilians and members of the civilian population are considered to be persons who do not directly participate in hostilities and members of belligerent forces who have laid down their arms or been prevented from fighting due to injury, illness, imprisonment or any other reason.

3. For the purposes of this article, the persons referred to in point (j) of Article 383 are protected by international law.

ARTICLE 387
(War crimes against combatant personnel)
Anyone who, in the context described in paragraph 1 of Article 385:
a) Compels a prisoner of war or of a belligerent force to serve in the armed forces of an enemy power or in the ranks of another belligerent force;
b) Deprives a prisoner of war or of a belligerent force or other person under the protection of international law of the right to a fair and impartial trial;

c) To condemn and execute, without prior trial by a regularly constituted court and offering the judicial guarantees generally recognized as indispensable, a prisoner of war or of a belligerent force or any person under the protection of international law;

d) To kill or wound a combatant who has laid down his arms or has unconditionally surrendered.

ARTICLE 386
(War crimes against property that is not a military objective)
Anyone who, under the conditions described in paragraph 1 of the previous article, is punishable by imprisonment for 3 to 12 years, unless a more severe penalty is applicable under another penal provision:
a) Attacking, in any way, population centers, dwellings or undefended buildings and, in general, civilian property or targets, causing their total or partial destruction or elimination, provided that such property or civilian targets do not constitute military objectives nor can those operations be justified by significant benefits or advantages of a military nature;
b) Looting conquered localities;
c) Attacking buildings dedicated to religious worship, education, the arts, science, assistance or charity, historical monuments, hospitals and other places where sick and wounded people are received and treated that are not military objectives;

d) Attacking buildings, equipment, units and medical vehicles,
identified with the emblems and distinctive signs of the Geneva Conventions of 1949, in accordance with international law;

e) Destroying or appropriating, in a massive and arbitrary manner, property,
whenever the destruction or appropriation is not justified by significant military needs.

ARTICLE 389
(Destruction of ships, aircraft or other civil transport)
Anyone who, during a war or armed conflict, destroys or seriously damages a civil ship or aircraft or civil vehicles for rail or road passenger transport, unnecessarily and without adopting, if necessary, the measures indispensable to preserving the safety of persons on board, is punishable by imprisonment from 2 to 10 years, unless a more severe penalty is applicable under another penal provision.

ARTICLE 388
(Other war crimes)
1. Anyone who, in the event of armed conflict, uses:
a) Atomic or radioactive weapons;
b) Poison or poisoned weapons;
c) Asphyxiating and toxic gases or any substance likely to cause death, illness or serious injury to an indeterminate number of people;
d) Bullets that expand or detonate inside the human body;
e) Weapons, projectiles, materials and methods of combat likely to cause, by their nature, superfluous injuries, unnecessary and indiscriminate suffering or effects, or designed to cause extensive, serious and lasting damage to the natural environment and endanger the health and survival of populations; A sentence of imprisonment from 3 to 20 years, unless a more severe penalty is applicable under another penal provision. 
f) Anyone who commits any other act classified as a war crime by international treaties or conventions signed by the Republic of Angola and incorporated into its domestic legal system shall be punished with the same penalty.

Article 136quater.

Section 1. The following constitute crimes under international law and are punishable in accordance with the provisions of this title: war crimes referred to in the Conventions adopted at Geneva on 12 August 1949 and in Additional Protocols I and II to those Conventions, adopted at Geneva on 8 June 1977, by the laws and customs applicable to armed conflicts, as defined in Article 2 of the Conventions adopted at Geneva on 12 August 1949, in Article 1 of Additional Protocols I and II adopted at Geneva on 8 June 1977, and in Article 8, paragraph 2(f) of the Statute of the International Criminal Court, and listed below, when these crimes infringe, by act or omission, the protection of persons and property guaranteed respectively by those Conventions, Protocols, laws, and customs:
without prejudice to the criminal provisions applicable to offenses committed by negligence:
1. Homicide intentional;

2. torture or other inhuman treatment, including biological experiments;

3. intentionally causing great suffering or serious injury to physical integrity or health;

4. rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, forced pregnancy, enforced sterilization, or any other form of sexual violence constituting a grave breach of the Geneva Conventions or a grave violation of Article 3 common to those Conventions;

5. other attacks on human dignity, in particular humiliating and degrading treatment;

6. compelling a prisoner of war, a civilian protected by the Convention for the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, or a person protected in this respect by Additional Protocols I and II to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949, to serve in the armed forces or armed groups of the enemy power or the opposing party;

7. The act of conscripting or enlisting children under the age of 15 into armed forces or armed groups, or of involving them actively in hostilities;

8. The act of depriving a prisoner of war, a civilian protected by the Convention for the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, or a person protected in this respect by Additional Protocols I and II to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949, of their right to a fair and due trial in accordance with the provisions of those instruments;

9. The unlawful deportation, transfer, or displacement, or unlawful detention, of a civilian protected by the Convention for the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War or a person protected in this respect by Additional Protocols I and II to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949;

10. The deliberate starvation of civilians as a method of warfare, by depriving them of goods indispensable to their survival, including by intentionally obstructing the delivery of aid as provided for in the Geneva Conventions;

11. Hostage-taking;

[...]
§ 2. The following constitute crimes under international law and shall be punished in accordance with the provisions of this Title: grave violations of Common Article 3 of the Geneva Conventions signed on 12 August 1949, in the event of armed conflict as defined by Common Article 3, and listed below, when such violations, by act or omission, undermine the protection of persons guaranteed by these Conventions, without prejudice to the penal provisions applicable to offenses committed by negligence:
1. Attacks on life and physical integrity, in particular murder in all its forms, mutilation, cruel treatment, and torture;

2. Violations of human dignity, including humiliating and degrading treatment;

3. Hostage-taking;

4. Sentences handed down and executions carried out without a prior judgment rendered by a duly constituted court, with the judicial guarantees generally recognized as essential.

sections.text_original:
Art. 136quater.
§ 1er. 
[...]
14° le fait de lancer des attaques délibérées contre des biens de caractère civil, c'est-à-dire des biens qui ne
sont pas des objectifs militaires;
15° le fait de lancer des attaques délibérées contre les bâtiments, le matériel, les unités et les moyens de
transport sanitaires, et le personnel utilisant, conformément au droit international, les signes distinctifs prévus
par le droit international humanitaire;
16° le fait d'utiliser la présence d'un civil ou d'une autre personne protégée par le droit international humanitaire
pour éviter que certains points, zones ou forces militaires ne soient la cible d'opérations militaires;
17° le fait de lancer des attaques délibérées contre le personnel, les installations, le matériel, les unités ou les
véhicules employés dans le cadre d'une mission humanitaire ou de maintien de la paix conformément à la Charte
des Nations unies, pour autant qu'ils aient droit à la protection que le droit international des conflits armés
garantit aux civils et aux biens de caractère civil;
18° les actes et omissions, non légalement justifiés, qui sont susceptibles de compromettre la santé et l'intégrité
physique ou mentale des personnes protégées par le droit international humanitaire, notamment tout acte
médical qui ne serait pas justifié par l'état de santé de ces personnes ou ne serait pas conforme aux règles de
l'art médical généralement reconnues;
19° sauf s'ils sont justifiés dans les conditions prévues au 18°, les actes consistant à pratiquer sur les
personnes visées au 18°, même avec leur consentement, des mutilations physiques, des expériences médicales
ou scientifiques ou des prélèvements de tissus ou d'organes pour des transplantations, à moins qu'il s'agisse de
dons de sang en vue de transfusions ou de dons de peau destinée à des greffes, pour autant que ces dons
soient volontaires, consentis et destinés à des fins thérapeutiques;
20° le fait de soumettre à une attaque délibérée la population civile ou des personnes civiles qui ne prennent
pas directement part aux hostilités;
21° le fait de lancer une attaque délibérée contre des lieux ou des malades et des blessés sont rassemblés pour
autant que ces lieux ne soient pas des objectifs militaires;
22° le fait de lancer une attaque délibérée en sachant que celle-ci causera des pertes en vies humaines, des
blessures aux personnes civiles ou des dommages aux biens de caractère civil ou des dommages étendus,
durables et graves à l'environnement naturel, qui seraient excessifs par rapport à l'avantage militaire concret et
direct attendu, sans préjudice de la criminalité de l'attaque dont les effets dommageables, même proportionnés à
l'avantage militaire attendu, seraient incompatibles avec les principes du droit des gens, tels qu'ils résultent des
usages établis, des principes de l'humanité et des exigences de la conscience publique;
23° le fait de lancer une attaque contre des ouvrages ou installations contenant des forces dangereuses, en
sachant que cette attaque causera des pertes en vies humaines, des blessures aux personnes civiles ou des
dommages aux biens de caractère civil, qui seraient excessifs par rapport à l'avantage militaire concret et direct
attendu, sans préjudice de la criminalité de l'attaque dont les effets dommageables même proportionnés à
l'avantage militaire attendu seraient incompatibles avec les principes du droit des gens, tels qu'ils résultent des
usages établis, des principes de l'humanité et des exigences de la conscience publique;
24° le fait de soumettre à une attaque ou de bombarder, par quelque moyen que ce soit, des zones
démilitarisées ou des villes, villages, habitations ou bâtiments non défendus qui ne sont pas des objectifs
militaires;
25° le pillage d'une ville ou d'une localité, même prise d'assaut;
26° le fait de soumettre une personne à une attaque en la sachant hors de combat à la condition que cette
attaque entraîne la mort ou des blessures;
27° le fait de tuer ou blesser par traîtrise des individus appartenant à la nation ou à l'armée ennemie ou un
adversaire combattant;
28° le fait de déclarer qu'il ne sera pas fait de quartier;
29° le fait d'utiliser perfidement le signe distinctif de la croix rouge ou du croissant rouge ou d'autres signes
protecteurs reconnus par le droit international humanitaire, à la condition que ce fait entraîne la mort ou des
blessures graves;
30° le fait d'utiliser indûment le pavillon parlementaire, le drapeau ou les insignes militaires et l'uniforme de
l'ennemi ou de l'Organisation des Nations unies, à la condition que ce fait entraîne la perte de vies humaines ou
des blessures graves;
31° le transfert, direct ou indirect, dans un territoire occupé d'une partie de la population civile de la puissance
occupante, dans le cas d'un conflit armé international, ou de l'autorité occupante dans le cas d'un conflit armé
non international;
32° le fait de retarder sans justification le rapatriement des prisonniers de guerre ou des civils;
33° le fait de se livrer aux pratiques de l'apartheid ou à d'autres pratiques inhumaines ou dégradantes fondées
sur la discrimination raciale et donnant lieu à des outrages à la dignité personnelle;
34° le fait de diriger des attaques contre les monuments historiques, les oeuvres d'art ou les lieux de culte
clairement reconnus qui constituent le patrimoine culturel ou spirituel des peuples et auxquels une protection
spéciale a été accordée en vertu d'un arrangement particulier alors qu'il n'existe aucune preuve de violation par la
partie adverse de l'interdiction d'utiliser ces biens à l'appui de l'effort militaire, et que ces biens ne sont pas situés
à proximité immédiate d'objectifs militaires;
35° le fait de lancer des attaques délibérées contre des bâtiments consacrés à la religion, à l'enseignement, à
l'art, à la science ou à l'action caritative, des monuments historiques, des hôpitaux, pour autant que ces
bâtiments ne soient pas des objectifs militaires;
36° le fait d'utiliser du poison ou des armes empoisonnées;
37° le fait d'utiliser des gaz asphyxiants, toxiques ou assimilés et tous liquides, matières ou engins analogues;
38° le fait d'utiliser des balles qui se dilatent ou s'aplatissent facilement dans le corps humain, telles que des
balles dont l'enveloppe dure ne recouvre pas entièrement le centre ou est percée d'entailles;
39° le fait de déclarer éteints, suspendus ou non recevables en justice les droits et actions des personnes
appartenant à la partie adverse;
40° le fait d'employer des armes, projectiles, matières et méthodes de guerre de nature à causer des maux
superflus ou des souffrances inutiles ou à frapper sans discrimination en violation du droit international des
conflits armés, à condition que ces armes, projectiles, matières et méthodes de guerre fassent l'objet d'une
interdiction générale et qu'ils soient inscrits dans une annexe au Statut de la Cour pénale internationale.
[1 41° le fait d'utiliser des armes qui utilisent des agents microbiens ou autres agents biologiques, ainsi que des
toxines, quels qu'en soient l'origine ou le mode de production;
42° le fait d'utiliser des armes ayant comme principal effet de blesser par des éclats qui ne sont pas localisables
par rayons X dans le corps humain;
43° le fait d'utiliser des armes à laser spécifiquement conçues de telle façon que leur seule fonction de combat
ou une de leurs fonctions de combat fût de provoquer la cécité permanente chez des personnes dont la vision
est non améliorée, c'est-à-dire qui regardent à l'oeil nu ou qui portent des dispositifs de correction de la vue.]

sections.text_english:
Article 136quater.

Section 1.

[...]
14. The act of launching deliberate attacks against civilian objects, that is to say, objects that are not military objectives;

15. The act of launching deliberate attacks against medical buildings, equipment, units and means of transport, and personnel using, in accordance with international law, the distinctive signs provided for by international humanitarian law;

16. The act of using the presence of a civilian or another person protected by international humanitarian law to prevent certain points, areas or military forces from being targeted by military operations;

17. Launching deliberate attacks against personnel, facilities, equipment, units, or vehicles employed in the context of a humanitarian or peacekeeping mission in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, insofar as they are entitled to the protection guaranteed to civilians and civilian objects under international humanitarian law;

18. Acts and omissions, not legally justified, that are likely to jeopardize the health and physical or mental integrity of persons protected by international humanitarian law, including any medical act that is not justified by the state of health of such persons or that does not conform to generally accepted medical practice;

19° Unless justified under the conditions set forth in 18°, acts consisting of performing physical mutilation, medical or scientific experiments, or the removal of tissues or organs for transplantation on the persons referred to in 18°, even with their consent, unless these are donations of blood for transfusion or donations of skin for grafting, provided that such donations are voluntary, consensual, and intended for therapeutic purposes;

20° The act of deliberately attacking the civilian population or civilians who are not directly taking part in hostilities;

21° The act of deliberately attacking places where the sick and wounded are gathered, provided that these places are not military objectives;

22. Launching a deliberate attack with the knowledge that it will cause loss of life, injury to civilians, damage to civilian property, or widespread, long-term, and severe damage to the natural environment, which would be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated, without prejudice to the criminality of the attack, the harmful effects of which, even if proportionate to the anticipated military advantage, would be incompatible with the principles of international law, as derived from established custom, principles of humanity, and the demands of public conscience;

23. Launching an attack against works or installations containing dangerous forces, knowing that the attack will cause loss of life, injury to civilians, or damage to civilian property, which would be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated, without prejudice to the criminality of the attack, the harmful effects of which, even if proportionate to the anticipated military advantage, would be incompatible with the principles of international law, as derived from established custom, the principles of humanity, and the demands of public conscience;

24. Subjecting to attack or bombarding, by any means whatsoever, demilitarized zones or undefended towns, villages, dwellings, or buildings that are not military objectives;

25. The looting of a town or locality, even if taken by storm;

26. The act of subjecting a person to an attack knowing them to be incapacitated, provided that such attack results in death or injury;

27. The act of treacherously killing or injuring individuals belonging to the enemy nation or army or an adversary in combat;

28. The act of declaring that no quarter will be given;

29. The act of treacherously using the distinctive emblem of the Red Cross or the Red Crescent or other protective emblems recognized by international humanitarian law, provided that such act results in death or serious injury;

30. The act of improperly using the parliamentary ensign, flag, or military insignia and the uniform of the enemy or of the United Nations, provided that such act results in loss of life or serious injury;

31° the transfer, direct or indirect, into an occupied territory of part of the civilian population of the occupying power, in the case of an international armed conflict, or of the occupying authority in the case of a non-international armed conflict;

32° the unjustified delay in the repatriation of prisoners of war or civilians;

33. Engaging in apartheid practices or other inhumane or degrading practices based on racial discrimination and resulting in outrages upon personal dignity;

34. Directing attacks against clearly recognized historical monuments, works of art, or places of worship that constitute the cultural or spiritual heritage of peoples and to which special protection has been granted under a specific arrangement, provided there is no evidence of a violation by the opposing party of the prohibition against using such property in support of the military effort, and such property is not located in the immediate vicinity of military objectives;

35. Launching deliberate attacks against buildings dedicated to religion, education, art, science, or charitable work, historical monuments, or hospitals, provided such buildings are not military objectives;

36. The use of poison or poisoned weapons;

37. The use of asphyxiating, toxic, or similar gases and any similar liquids, substances, or devices;

38. The use of bullets that expand or flatten easily in the human body, such as
bullet whose hard casing does not completely cover the center or is pierced with notches;

39. The act of declaring extinguished, suspended, or inadmissible in court the rights and actions of persons
belonging to the opposing party;

40. The use of weapons, projectiles, materials, and methods of warfare capable of causing superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering, or of striking indiscriminately, in violation of international armed conflict law, provided that such weapons, projectiles, materials, and methods of warfare are subject to a general prohibition and are listed in an annex to the Statute of the International Criminal Court.

41. The use of weapons that employ microbial or other biological agents, as well as toxins, regardless of their origin or method of production;

42. The use of weapons whose principal effect is to cause injury by means of fragments that cannot be located by X-ray in the human body;

43° the use of laser weapons specifically designed in such a way that their sole combat function
or one of their combat functions was to cause permanent blindness in persons whose vision
is not enhanced, i.e., who see with the naked eye or who wear corrective lenses.]

Article 136septies.

The following are punishable by the penalty prescribed for the completed offense:
1° the order, even if not carried out, to commit one of the offenses provided for in Articles 136bis, 136ter, and 136quater;

2° the proposal or offer to commit such an offense and the acceptance of such a proposal or offer;

3° incitement to commit such an offense, even if not carried out;

4° participation, within the meaning of Articles 66 and 67, in such an offense, even if not carried out;

5° the failure to act within the limits of their ability to act by those who were aware of orders given for the commission of such an offense or of acts that commence its commission, and who could have prevented its completion or brought it to an end;

6. Attempting to commit such an offense, as defined in Articles 51 to 53.

Art. 136octies.

§ 1. Without prejudice to the exceptions set forth in points 18, 22, and 23 of Article 136quater, § 1, no political, military, or national interest or necessity may justify the offenses defined in Articles 136bis, 136ter, 136quater, 136sexies, and 136septies, even if committed in reprisal.

§ 2. The fact that the accused acted on the orders of his government or a superior does not exempt him from his
responsibility if, in the given circumstances, the order could clearly lead to the commission of one of the
offenses referred to in Articles 136bis, 136ter and 136quater.

Article 136quater.

Section 1. The following constitute crimes under international law and are punishable in accordance with the provisions of this title: war crimes referred to in the Conventions adopted at Geneva on 12 August 1949 and in Additional Protocols I and II to those Conventions, adopted at Geneva on 8 June 1977, by the laws and customs applicable to armed conflicts, as defined in Article 2 of the Conventions adopted at Geneva on 12 August 1949, in Article 1 of Additional Protocols I and II adopted at Geneva on 8 June 1977, and in Article 8, paragraph 2(f) of the Statute of the International Criminal Court, and listed below, when these crimes infringe, by act or omission, the protection of persons and property guaranteed respectively by those Conventions, Protocols, laws, and customs:
without prejudice to the criminal provisions applicable to offenses committed by negligence:
1. Homicide intentional;

2. torture or other inhuman treatment, including biological experiments;

3. intentionally causing great suffering or serious injury to physical integrity or health;

4. rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, forced pregnancy, enforced sterilization, or any other form of sexual violence constituting a grave breach of the Geneva Conventions or a grave violation of Article 3 common to those Conventions;

5. other attacks on human dignity, in particular humiliating and degrading treatment;

6. compelling a prisoner of war, a civilian protected by the Convention for the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, or a person protected in this respect by Additional Protocols I and II to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949, to serve in the armed forces or armed groups of the enemy power or the opposing party;

7. The act of conscripting or enlisting children under the age of 15 into armed forces or armed groups, or of involving them actively in hostilities;

8. The act of depriving a prisoner of war, a civilian protected by the Convention for the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War, or a person protected in this respect by Additional Protocols I and II to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949, of their right to a fair and due trial in accordance with the provisions of those instruments;

9. The unlawful deportation, transfer, or displacement, or unlawful detention, of a civilian protected by the Convention for the Protection of Civilian Persons in Time of War or a person protected in this respect by Additional Protocols I and II to the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949;

10. The deliberate starvation of civilians as a method of warfare, by depriving them of goods indispensable to their survival, including by intentionally obstructing the delivery of aid as provided for in the Geneva Conventions;

11. Hostage-taking;

[...]
§ 2. The following constitute crimes under international law and shall be punished in accordance with the provisions of this Title: grave violations of Common Article 3 of the Geneva Conventions signed on 12 August 1949, in the event of armed conflict as defined by Common Article 3, and listed below, when such violations, by act or omission, undermine the protection of persons guaranteed by these Conventions, without prejudice to the penal provisions applicable to offenses committed by negligence:
1. Attacks on life and physical integrity, in particular murder in all its forms, mutilation, cruel treatment, and torture;

2. Violations of human dignity, including humiliating and degrading treatment;

3. Hostage-taking;

4. Sentences handed down and executions carried out without a prior judgment rendered by a duly constituted court, with the judicial guarantees generally recognized as essential.

