Article 223
For the purposes of this Act, “war crimes” means:
1. grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949, namely any of the following acts when committed against persons or property protected by provisions of the Geneva Conventions:
(a) willful killing;
(b) torture or inhuman, cruel, or degrading treatment or punishment, including biological experiments;
(c) the intentional causing of great suffering or serious injury to physical, mental, or health;
(d) the destruction or appropriation of property not justified by military necessity and carried out on a large scale in an unlawful and arbitrary manner;
(e) compelling a prisoner of war or a protected person to serve in the forces of an enemy power;

(f) Intentionally depriving a prisoner of war or any other protected person of their right to a fair and due trial; (g) unlawful deportation or transfer or unlawful detention; (h) hostage-taking;

2. Other serious violations of the laws and customs applicable to international armed conflicts within the established framework of international law, namely, any of the following: (a) Intentionally directing attacks against the civilian population as such or against civilians not directly participating in hostilities; (b) Intentionally directing attacks against civilian objects, that is, objects that are not military objectives;

(c) Intentionally directing attacks against personnel, facilities, equipment, units, or vehicles employed in humanitarian assistance or peacekeeping operations in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations or the Charter of the African Union, insofar as they are entitled to the protection guaranteed to civilians and civilian objects under international law of armed conflict;

(d) Intentionally directing an attack knowing that it will incidentally cause civilian casualties, injuries to civilians, damage to civilian objects, or widespread, long-term, and severe damage to the natural environment that would be manifestly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated; (e) Attacking or bombarding, by any means whatsoever, towns, villages, dwellings, or buildings that are not defended and are not military objectives; (f) Killing or wounding a combatant who, having laid down their arms or having no means of self-defense, has surrendered; (g) Improperly using the parliamentary ensign, flag, or military insignia and uniform of the enemy or of the United Nations, the African Union, or any other international organization, as well as the distinctive signs provided for by the Geneva Conventions, and thereby causing loss of life or serious injury; (h) the transfer, direct or indirect, by an occupying power of part of the civilian population within the territory it occupies, or the deportation or transfer within or outside the occupied territory of all or part of the population of that territory;

(i) the intentional direction of attacks against buildings used for religious, educational, cultural, artistic, scientific, or charitable purposes, historical monuments, hospitals, and places where the sick or wounded are gathered, provided they are not military objectives;

(j) subjecting persons of an opposing party who have fallen into its power to mutilation or to any medical or scientific experiments whatsoever that are not motivated by medical, dental, or hospital treatment, nor carried out in the interest of those persons, and that result in their death or seriously endanger their health; (k) killing or wounding by treachery any person belonging to the enemy nation or army; (l) declaring that no quarter will be given; (m) destroying or seizing the property of the enemy, except where such destruction or seizure is imperatively required by the necessities of war; (n) declaring extinguished, suspended, or inadmissible in court the rights and actions of nationals of the opposing party; (o) the act of a belligerent compelling nationals of the opposing party to take part in war operations directed against their country, even if they were in the service of that belligerent before the start of the war; (p) the pillaging of a city or town, even if taken by storm; (q) the use of poison.
(u) poisoned weapons;

(r) the use of asphyxiating, toxic, or similar gases, liquids, substances, or similar processes;

(s) the use of bullets that expand or flatten easily in the human body, such as bullets whose hard casing does not completely cover the center or is pierced with notches;

(t) the use of weapons, projectiles, materials, and methods of warfare that are likely to cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering, or to strike indiscriminately, in violation of international law on armed conflict, provided that such weapons, projectiles, materials, and methods of warfare are subject to a general prohibition and are included in an annex to the Statute of the International Criminal Court by way of amendment adopted in accordance with Articles 121 and 123;

(u) attacks on human dignity,
in particular humiliating and
degrading treatment;

(v) rape, sexual slavery, forced prostitution,
forced pregnancy as defined in Article 222, paragraph 1, point 7 of this Penal Code,
on crimes against humanity, forced sterilization,
or any other form of sexual assault or
violence constituting a grave breach of the Geneva Conventions;

(w) using the presence of a civilian or
other protected person to prevent
certain points, areas, or military forces from being targeted by military operations;

(x) intentionally launching attacks against buildings, equipment, units, and
medical transport, and against
personnel using, in accordance with international law, the distinctive signs provided for by the Geneva Conventions;

(y) the deliberate starvation of civilians as a method of warfare, by depriving them of goods essential to their survival, including by intentionally obstructing the delivery of aid as provided for in the Geneva Conventions;

(z) the conscription or enlistment of children under the age of 15 into the armed forces or their active participation in hostilities.
